# core/identity-audit - Which elements are linked across sources?
#
# Answers: elements carrying a cross-source identity assertion, which kind it is,
# and what it points at.
#
# Does NOT prove: that unlisted elements have no counterpart elsewhere. Identity
# across tools cannot be derived - the same application in ArchiMate and in
# Backstage produces two IRIs by construction - so it is authored and
# human-reviewed. An absent assertion means nobody has made it yet.
#
# REQUIRES capabilities.identity_assertions, which is false for raw converter
# output: the converters never emit identity. Under the default profile this is
# refused, and that refusal is the correct answer to "join these two models" - the
# work is a reconciliation step, not a cleverer query.
#
# The two predicates differ in force and the distinction is expensive to get wrong.
# skos:exactMatch asserts correspondence between records; owl:sameAs asserts the
# resources ARE one thing, which merges everything said about both and is
# unforgiving of a mistake. Prefer the first. ?kind keeps them apart here.
#
# When a cross-notation join comes back empty, run this before rewriting SPARQL.
# The cause is almost always a missing assertion rather than a wrong query.
# Labels are read in SEPARATE semantic scopes, and the two sides use different
# ones. Two reasons, both observed rather than theorised. The assertions live
# wherever the reconciliation step put them - typically their own graph - while
# labels live with the elements, so reading both in one scope binds the graph
# variable to the reconciliation graph and returns every label empty. And the whole
# point of a cross-source assertion is that the two elements come from different
# models, so they sit in different semantic graphs: `semantic2` opens an
# independent scope on the same role, which is what stops the counterpart's label
# from being required to live in the element's graph.
#
# DISTINCT because the scope is every graph, and one assertion repeated across graphs is
# still one assertion. Converter output does repeat statements between graphs, so without
# it a handful of identity links can arrive as a page of rows and read as a well-reconciled
# dataset - the opposite of what the count means. Measured on a large export: 215 rows from
# 4 owl:sameAs assertions.
#
# What DISTINCT deliberately does NOT collapse is direction. If A and B assert identity of
# each other, both rows stand: a reciprocal pair and a one-sided assertion are different
# findings, and the one-sided case is the one worth chasing. Reading the pair as
# duplication would hide it.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT DISTINCT ?element ?elementLabel ?kind ?counterpart ?counterpartLabel
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:any}}
    {
      ?element {{ROLE:same_as}} ?counterpart .
      BIND("sameAs (asserts one thing - merges both descriptions)" AS ?kind)
    }
    UNION
    {
      ?element {{ROLE:exact_match}} ?counterpart .
      BIND("exactMatch (asserts correspondence between records)" AS ?kind)
    }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  OPTIONAL {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
      ?element {{PATH:label}} ?elementLabel .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
  OPTIONAL {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic2}}
      ?counterpart {{PATH:label}} ?counterpartLabel .
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
}
ORDER BY ?kind ?elementLabel
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
